# Copy this file to .env and adjust as needed for local development.
# docker-compose.yml reads these values for the postgres service.

POSTGRES_DB=vision
POSTGRES_USER=vision
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=vision
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# LAN WebRTC/WHEP viewing (docs/plans/done/MVP2-PLAN.md V-e). mediamtx advertises this
# host in its ICE candidates so a browser can actually reach WebRTC media --
# left unset, WHEP only ever works for a browser on this same machine
# (defaults to 127.0.0.1, docker-compose.yml's own prior hardcoded value).
# Set it to this machine's LAN IP so phones/laptops on the LAN get
# sub-second WebRTC instead of always falling back to HLS, e.g.:
#   VISION_WEBRTC_HOST=192.168.0.104
# Find your LAN IP with `hostname -I` (Linux), `ipconfig getifaddr en0`
# (macOS), or `ipconfig` (Windows, look for "IPv4 Address"). mediamtx's own
# env-var parsing splits list-typed fields on commas (verified against the
# maintainer's own confirmation, github.com/bluenviron/mediamtx discussions
# #3360), so you can comma-separate multiple hosts, e.g.
# "127.0.0.1,192.168.0.104", if you want both same-host and LAN viewers to
# work at once -- a bare single value replaces the default 127.0.0.1
# outright rather than adding to it. Viewers off this LAN (or anyone this
# isn't configured for) transparently fall back to HLS, which has no such
# reachability limitation. Uncomment and edit the line below, or leave it
# unset for same-host-only WHEP:
# VISION_WEBRTC_HOST=192.168.0.104

# Worker-pull RTSP base (docs/plans/done/MEDIA-SOT-PLAN.md §5.5, wave M7). Only matters once
# vision.cv.frame-transport=pull is turned on (off by default -- unused, and harmless to leave set,
# while the deployment stays on the default push transport). This is the address the CV WORKER
# (cv-service) dials, not this app -- defaults to docker-compose.yml's own compose-internal
# "rtsp://mediamtx:8554", which cv-service's own container can already resolve on this same compose
# network. Set it to this machine's LAN address (find it the same way as VISION_WEBRTC_HOST above)
# when cv-service runs on a REMOTE box that cannot resolve compose-internal hostnames at all -- e.g.
# the GB4005 Intel/OpenVINO inference box (see cv/cv-service/DEPLOY-GPU.md), which is exactly why this
# property is kept separate from vision.publish.mediamtx.rtsp-base (that one stays "mediamtx", this
# one needs a real reachable address for an off-box worker):
# VISION_CV_PULL_RTSP_BASE=rtsp://192.168.0.104:8554

# mediamtx Control API credentials for a REMOTE mediamtx (docs/plans/done/MEDIA-SOT-PLAN.md §5.3,
# wave M7 + its follow-up hardening). Only matters once vision.publish.source-proxy.enabled=true is
# turned on (off by default -- unused, and harmless to leave set, while that switch stays off, D1).
#
# This docker-compose.yml always runs mediamtx and vision-app as sibling containers on the SAME
# Docker host, so it doesn't need these: the mediamtx.yml this repo mounts widens mediamtx's own
# Control API auth (authInternalUsers) to allow any caller on the private compose network, and
# docker-compose.yml's own host port mapping for that API is loopback-only
# (127.0.0.1:19997:9997) precisely so that widening is never reachable from the LAN or internet --
# see docker-compose.yml's "Control API" port comment for the incident that fixed. Neither of those
# two facts holds if you deploy mediamtx on a DIFFERENT host than vision-app (not a shape this
# compose file sets up on its own): a remote mediamtx's loopback binding does nothing for a caller
# on another machine, so ips: [] widening -- safe only on a network as trusted as one compose
# stack's private bridge -- would mean unauthenticated path create/repoint/delete for anyone who can
# reach that host at all. For that topology, configure a REAL user in the remote mediamtx's own
# authInternalUsers (a username/password, not another ips: [] entry) and point vision-app at it with
# an explicit Control API base plus those credentials, e.g.:
#   VISION_PUBLISH_MEDIAMTX_API_BASE=http://192.168.0.104:19997
#   VISION_PUBLISH_MEDIAMTX_API_USER=vision
#   VISION_PUBLISH_MEDIAMTX_API_PASSWORD=<a real secret, not committed anywhere>
# These three map onto vision.publish.mediamtx.api-base/api-user/api-password (see
# MediamtxControlApi and VisionPublishProperties.Mediamtx, adapter-publish-hls / vision-app) via
# Spring's standard relaxed env-var binding -- the same convention as every other
# VISION_PUBLISH_MEDIAMTX_* property already wired in docker-compose.yml (rtsp-base/hls-base/
# whep-base). Neither var is read by this compose file today (source-proxy stays off, and mediamtx
# is always co-located here); they're documented for a deployment that departs from this file, not
# as something to uncomment for the default demo stack.
